// =====================================================================
// FILE: firestore.rules
// =====================================================================
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS
//   The security rules for your database. Your Firebase settings are
//   visible to anyone (they're in the browser), so ANYONE could try to
//   read or write your database. These rules decide what's allowed.
//
// ✅ This file is FILLED IN for you. Your job:
//   1. Read the explanation below.
//   2. Copy everything from `rules_version` to the last `}` at the bottom.
//   3. Firebase console → Firestore Database → Rules tab → delete what's
//      there → paste → click Publish.
//   The React app never reads this file. It's your saved copy of the rules.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// FIRST: how Firestore stores data
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//   collection "messages"            ≈ a table (a folder of messages)
//     document "aB3x..." (auto id)   ≈ one row (one message)
//       { content: "hi", userId: "...", userEmail: "...", createdAt: <time> }
//   There's no "create table" step. The collection appears by itself the
//   first time your app saves a message.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT EACH LINE BELOW MEANS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
//   • rules_version / service cloud.firestore / match /databases/...
//     → the standard wrapper every Firestore rules file starts with.
//       Always the same; you don't need to understand it.
//   • match /messages/{messageId}
//     → "these rules are for every document in the messages collection".
//       {messageId} stands for "any id".
//   • allow read: if request.auth != null;
//     → you can read messages only if you're logged in.
//       request.auth is the logged-in user; null means logged out.
//       This also controls onSnapshot: logged-out people get nothing.
//   • allow create: if ...
//     → you can ADD a message only if ALL of these are true (&& = "and"):
//         - you're logged in
//         - request.resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid
//           request.resource.data = the message you're trying to save.
//           Its userId must be YOUR id, so nobody can post pretending
//           to be someone else.
//         - content is text, not empty, and at most 1000 characters
//   • allow update, delete: if false;
//     → nobody can edit or delete messages (false = never).
//
// =====================================================================

rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    match /messages/{messageId} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;

      allow create: if request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid
        && request.resource.data.content is string
        && request.resource.data.content.size() > 0
        && request.resource.data.content.size() <= 1000;

      allow update, delete: if false;
    }
  }
}
